/-!
# Toric comparison of ideals, saturated blowups and smoothness

Source: ILO, Travaux de Gabber, Exposé VIII, 3.1.17--3.1.23, 3.2.9, 3.2.12,
3.2.14--3.2.19 (blueprint: lem-trf-toric-monoid-comparison).

All comparisons retain their maps to the base. Equivariance is expressed by the unique
lifts of the original torus action to each modification; dense torus embeddings are
specified over the original embedding. No arbitrary comparison map is substituted.
-/

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits TopologicalSpace Opposite

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Monoscheme

/-- The product of a component torus with the inverse image of that component.
Source: ILO VIII 3.2.4, 3.2.17--3.2.18 (blueprint: lem-trf-toric-monoid-comparison). -/
noncomputable def toricActionDomain (k : Type u) [Field k] {M : Fine.{u}}
    (C : ToricComponent M) {Y : Scheme.{u}} (f : Y ⟶ realization k M) : Scheme.{u} :=
  pullback (torusToBase k C) (pullback.snd f (componentι k C) ≫ componentToBase k C)

/-- The original action composed with the map from the product over the modification.
Source: ILO VIII 3.2.4, 3.2.17--3.2.18 (blueprint: lem-trf-toric-monoid-comparison). -/
noncomputable def toricActionBase (k : Type u) [Field k] {M : Fine.{u}}
    (C : ToricComponent M) {Y : Scheme.{u}} (f : Y ⟶ realization k M) :
    toricActionDomain k C f ⟶ realization k M :=
  pullback.lift (pullback.fst _ _)
    (pullback.snd _ _ ≫ pullback.snd f (componentι k C))
    (by rw [Category.assoc]; exact pullback.condition) ≫ torusAction k C ≫ componentι k C

/-- Equivariance of an isomorphism over k[M], with both lifted actions pinned over the
original action. Uniqueness makes these the induced torus actions. The three projection
equations specify the product map induced by the isomorphism, with no chosen alternative.
Source: ILO VIII 3.2.17--3.2.18 (blueprint: lem-trf-toric-monoid-comparison). -/
def IsToricComparison (k : Type u) [Field k] {M : Fine.{u}} {Y Z : Scheme.{u}}
    (f : Y ⟶ realization k M) (g : Z ⟶ realization k M) (e : Y ≅ Z) : Prop :=
  e.hom ≫ g = f ∧ ∀ C : ToricComponent M,
    (∃! μ : toricActionDomain k C f ⟶ Y, μ ≫ f = toricActionBase k C f) ∧
    (∃! μ : toricActionDomain k C g ⟶ Z, μ ≫ g = toricActionBase k C g) ∧
    (∀ (μ : toricActionDomain k C f ⟶ Y) (ν : toricActionDomain k C g ⟶ Z),
      μ ≫ f = toricActionBase k C f → ν ≫ g = toricActionBase k C g →
      ∃ a : toricActionDomain k C f ⟶ toricActionDomain k C g,
        a ≫ pullback.fst _ _ = pullback.fst _ _ ∧
        a ≫ pullback.snd _ _ ≫ pullback.fst _ _ =
          pullback.snd _ _ ≫ pullback.fst _ _ ≫ e.hom ∧
        a ≫ pullback.snd _ _ ≫ pullback.snd _ _ =
          pullback.snd _ _ ≫ pullback.snd _ _ ∧ μ ≫ e.hom = a ≫ ν) ∧
    ∃ (j : torus k C ⟶ Y) (l : torus k C ⟶ Z),
      IsOpenImmersion j ∧ IsOpenImmersion l ∧
      j ≫ f = torusEmbedding k C ∧ l ≫ g = torusEmbedding k C ∧ j ≫ e.hom = l ∧
      closure (Set.range j) = f ⁻¹' (componentOpen k C : Set (realization k M)) ∧
      closure (Set.range l) = g ⁻¹' (componentOpen k C : Set (realization k M))

/-- The ideal comparison includes the empty ideal, localization, arbitrary pullback,
unit ideals and nonvanishing on each connected component.
Source: ILO VIII 3.1.18, 3.2.14--3.2.15 (blueprint: lem-trf-toric-monoid-comparison).
Proof (node steps 1--3): on a chart k[P], localize e^p to the same monomial in
k[P[a^-1]], using e^(p-na) ↦ e^p/(e^a)^n for the inverse. For an equivariant ideal,
apply the coefficient functional at each lattice exponent to its coaction to obtain
c_p e^p in the ideal. Divide by nonzero c_p to recover every monomial in its support.
The exponents form an ideal, and this is inverse to I ↦ k[I]. Collect the finite
supports of finitely many ring generators to prove coherence in the reverse direction.
These inverse formulas commute with restriction and glue. Extension along P → Q gives
the ideal generated by the image exponents on both sides, proving pullback, hence also
localization. Linear independence of monomials gives the unit and nonzero assertions.
Zero generators give the empty monoid ideal and the zero ring ideal. -/
theorem toric_ideal_comparison (k : Type u) [Field k] (M : Fine.{u})
    (hM : M.IsTorsionFree) :
    (∀ I : IdealFamily M, IsCoherentIdeal I →
      (∀ U : (realization k M).affineOpens, ((ringIdeal k I).ideal U).FG) ∧
      IsTorusEquivariant k M (ringIdeal k I)) ∧
    (∀ J : (realization k M).IdealSheafData,
      (∀ U : (realization k M).affineOpens, (J.ideal U).FG) → IsTorusEquivariant k M J →
      ∃! I : IdealFamily M, IsCoherentIdeal I ∧ ringIdeal k I = J) ∧
    (∀ (I I' : IdealFamily M), IsCoherentIdeal I → IsCoherentIdeal I' →
      ringIdeal k I = ringIdeal k I' → I = I') ∧
    ringIdeal k (emptyIdeal M) = ⊥ ∧
    (∀ I : IdealFamily M, IsCoherentIdeal I →
      (ringIdeal k I = ⊤ ↔ I = wholeIdeal M) ∧
      ∀ C : ToricComponent M,
        (ringIdeal k I).comap (componentι k C) ≠ ⊥ ↔
          ∃ x : M.space, ConnectedComponents.mk x = C.component ∧
            (idealStalk I x).Nonempty) ∧
    (∀ (N : Fine.{u}) (f : Hom N M) (I : IdealFamily M), IsCoherentIdeal I →
      IsCoherentIdeal (pullbackIdeal f I) ∧
      ringIdeal k (pullbackIdeal f I) = (ringIdeal k I).comap (realizationMap k f)) := by
  sorry

/-- Saturated blowups exist, remain fine and torsion free with finite affine covers,
and identify canonically with normalized scheme blowups, equivariantly and with the
original dense torus unchanged. The last clause is naturality and the pullback assertion
for smooth maps; open localizations are included among those maps.
Source: ILO VIII 3.1.17--3.1.23, 3.2.17--3.2.18
(blueprint: lem-trf-toric-monoid-comparison).
Proof (node steps 4--5 and 7): choose finite nonempty chart generators a of I. The ordinary
scheme blowup chart k[P][k[I]/e^a] is k[P[I-a]]. The monomial formulas agree after
inverting e^(b-a) on overlaps. Apply lem-trf-affine-monoid-algebra-normalization to
identify its normalization with k[P[I-a]^sat]. Finite saturation gives fine charts,
torsion-free unchanged group completions, and finitely many charts. Glue them, using
localization compatibility of saturation. This constructs the saturated monoscheme
blowup and the comparison over the base. Invert all monomials: both maps become the
identity on the original torus. The coactions on these charts agree, so the lifted
actions and their comparison are equivariant. Dense-torus agreement proves uniqueness.
For smooth pullback, smallest affine neighborhoods have Q=P×N^r×Z^t. The extended
blowup charts and their saturations are the original charts times N^r×Z^t. Their ring
algebras are the same polynomial/Laurent extensions. This gives the pullback square
and the commuting comparison square, including identity and composite pullbacks. -/
theorem toric_blowup_comparison (k : Type u) [Field k] (M : Fine.{u})
    (hM : M.IsTorsionFree) (I : IdealFamily M) (hI : IsNonemptyCoherentIdeal I) :
    (∃ (B : Fine.{u}) (b : Hom B M), IsSaturatedBlowup b I) ∧
    ∀ (B : Fine.{u}) (b : Hom B M), IsSaturatedBlowup b I →
      B.IsTorsionFree ∧
      (∃! e : realization k B ≅ (ringIdeal k I).normalizedBlowup,
        e.hom ≫ (ringIdeal k I).normalizedBlowupMap = realizationMap k b) ∧
      (∀ e : realization k B ≅ (ringIdeal k I).normalizedBlowup,
        e.hom ≫ (ringIdeal k I).normalizedBlowupMap = realizationMap k b →
        IsToricComparison k (realizationMap k b) (ringIdeal k I).normalizedBlowupMap e) ∧
      ∀ (N : Fine.{u}) (f : Hom N M), f.IsSmooth →
        ∀ (B' : Fine.{u}) (b' : Hom B' N),
          IsSaturatedBlowup b' (pullbackIdeal f I) →
          ∃ (g : Hom B' B)
            (q : (ringIdeal k (pullbackIdeal f I)).normalizedBlowup ⟶
              (ringIdeal k I).normalizedBlowup),
            g.map ≫ b.map = b'.map ≫ f.map ∧
            IsPullback q (ringIdeal k (pullbackIdeal f I)).normalizedBlowupMap
              (ringIdeal k I).normalizedBlowupMap (realizationMap k f) ∧
            ∀ (e : realization k B ≅ (ringIdeal k I).normalizedBlowup)
              (e' : realization k B' ≅ (ringIdeal k (pullbackIdeal f I)).normalizedBlowup),
              e.hom ≫ (ringIdeal k I).normalizedBlowupMap = realizationMap k b →
              e'.hom ≫ (ringIdeal k (pullbackIdeal f I)).normalizedBlowupMap =
                realizationMap k b' → e'.hom ≫ q = realizationMap k g ≫ e.hom := by
  sorry

/-- Smooth monoscheme morphisms realize to smooth scheme morphisms, without requiring
torsion-freeness of either monoscheme.
Source: ILO VIII 3.1.13--3.1.16, 3.2.12(i) (blueprint: lem-trf-toric-monoid-comparison).
Proof (node step 6): each point has a smallest affine neighborhood, obtained by inverting
the product of those finite monoid generators outside its prime. Thus a smooth stalk
splitting gives an actual neighborhood chart P → P×N^r×Z^t. Its monoid algebra is a
polynomial/Laurent extension of k[P], which is smooth: lift polynomial variables freely
and Laurent variables as units across square-zero extensions. A lift of a unit is a
unit because 1+n has inverse 1-n for n²=0. Apply smoothness local on the source. -/
theorem realizationMap_smooth (k : Type u) [Field k] {N M : Fine.{u}}
    (f : Hom N M) (hf : f.IsSmooth) : Smooth (realizationMap k f) := by
  sorry

/-- Smoothness of a torsion-free toric realization reflects monoscheme smoothness.
Source: ILO VIII 3.2.12(ii) (blueprint: lem-trf-toric-monoid-comparison).
Proof (node steps 8--12): restrict to affine charts. Smoothness gives normality and
regularity (lem-trf-smooth-geometrically-normal). Finite toric normalization and
lem-trf-finite-birational-normal-iso imply P=P^sat by monomial independence. Split off
units: face sharpening makes the sharp group completion finite free, so lift a basis
to split P as its units times its sharp quotient R. The unit group is finite free
(lem-trf-fg-torsionfree-group-free). A minimal finite generating set of R consists of
its atoms: decomposing a generator would express it using the others, since sharpness
and cancellation forbid its recurrence. At the rational torus-fixed point, cotangent
vectors are the atom monomials and y_j-1. They are independent by mapping into a
square-zero algebra with these distinct basis vectors. The finite-type domain dimension
is rank(P^gp), by lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-domain-dimension-trdeg. The regular local
cotangent criterion equates this with the number of atoms plus the unit rank. Thus the
atoms are a basis of R^gp and freely generate R. Every stalk is N^r×Z^t. -/
theorem isSmooth_of_realization_smooth (k : Type u) [Field k] (M : Fine.{u})
    (hM : M.IsTorsionFree) (h : Smooth (realizationToBase k M)) : M.IsSmooth := by
  sorry

/-- A regular rational toric realization has free monoid times lattice stalks.
Source: ILO VIII 3.2.19 (blueprint: lem-trf-toric-monoid-comparison).
Proof (node step 13): the monomials of finitely many monoid generators show that the
rational realization is locally of finite type over Q. This field is perfect, so
lem-trf-regular-perfect-field-smooth gives smoothness. Apply the preceding reflection
theorem. This includes the empty monoscheme, trivial monoids and arbitrary torus ranks.
ULift changes only the universe of the rational field. -/
theorem isSmooth_of_rational_realization_regular (M : Fine.{u})
    (hM : M.IsTorsionFree) (h : IsRegularScheme (realization (ULift.{u} ℚ) M)) :
    ∀ x : M.space, ∃ r t : ℕ, Nonempty
      ((FineFan.stalk M.space.presheaf x) ≃*
        (Multiplicative (Fin r → ℕ) × Multiplicative (Fin t → ℤ))) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Monoscheme
